course: dictionary
lesson: 11223
title: Woodpile and its clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word becomes clear when you read the words around it. These are its context, and they leave clues. «Lift a log from the woodpile and you can count its age.» Logs are pieces of wood, so a woodpile must be a pile of wood, not a pile of stones.
explain: «Stack the woodpile before the frost comes.» Stack is a clue. You stack things by putting them in a neat heap, so a woodpile is a neat heap of logs or chopped wood, usually kept outside for a fire or stove.
explain: Context can also warn you. «In North America ... do not put your hands into woodpiles or dark corners.» Here the clue is danger: creatures can hide in a woodpile. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does "a neat heap of logs" still make sense? Yes, so you have it.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Woodpile» means a neat heap or stack of logs or chopped wood.
fact: A woodpile is usually kept outside, near a house, cabin or campsite, for a fireplace, wood stove or campfire.
fact: «Woodpile» can also mean a hidden problem, as in the old saying «something hiding in the woodpile».
fact: Woodpiles can be homes for insects, spiders, mice, lizards and snakes, so wear gloves and look before you reach in.
exercise: «Lift a log from the woodpile and you can count its age.» What is a woodpile? | *A neat heap of logs or chopped wood, kept outside for burning | Tall trees with many branches | A fire lit in an open field
exercise: «Stack the woodpile before the frost comes.» In this sentence, what does stack most likely mean? | Paint | Throw away | *Pile up neatly | Hide
exercise: Why do people in North America keep their hands out of woodpiles? | Woodpiles are full of water | *Black widows and brown recluses may hide there and can give serious bites | Woodpiles are too hot to touch | Logs are too heavy to lift
test: An old saying says «something hiding in the woodpile». What does it mean? | A mouse with a long tail | A ring of wood from a tree | A pile of firewood waiting to be burned | *A problem that is hidden and not yet noticed
test: What do the rings in a log show? | Pale logs are always younger than dark ones | *Each year the tree adds one ring of wood, pale in spring and darker in late summer | Logs get older each time they are split | The age is painted on the bark
exam: Ella wants to reach into the woodpile for a log; what should she do first? | Light a campfire beside the woodpile | Wet the logs with water | *Put on gloves and look carefully before she reaches in | Grab the log quickly before anything can bite
